# Expectation record for 01_integer_conversions/008_unsigned_wraparound.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 008_unsigned_wraparound
area               = 01_integer_conversions
description        = Modular wraparound of unsigned types at and across their maximum, folded and runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Unsigned arithmetic is defined to wrap modulo 2^N, so every expression in this program is
well defined rather than undefined, which is precisely what makes wraparound a legitimate
subject for byte-exact comparison. No signed type ever overflows: the two narrow cases add
1 to 255 and to 65535 in promoted int, giving 256 and 65536, and are then explicitly cast
down. No shift operator appears anywhere. No aliasing violation, no uninitialized read, no
object modified twice between sequence points, at most one side-effecting argument per call,
no dependence on padding bytes.
Every narrowing is written as an explicit cast, which keeps the strict warning gate clean
without any deviation, and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer reports nothing because none of these
operations is undefined.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
unsigned long is deliberately never used. Its width is 4 bytes on i686 and 8 bytes on the
other three targets, so an expression such as 0uL - 1uL would print two different values
across backends and would be reported as a cross-backend divergence that says nothing about
the compiler. Only unsigned int, which is 32 bits on all four supported targets, and
unsigned long long, which is 64 bits on all four, are used for the wide cases, alongside
unsigned char and unsigned short for the narrow ones.
No header is included, so every limit is spelled literally as 4294967295u, ~0u and
18446744073709551615uLL rather than taken from limits.h or stdint.h.
Plain char is never used and no object is declared plain char, because plain char signedness
was measured signed on x86-64 and i686 but unsigned on AArch64 and RISC-V 64. No pointer
value is printed.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
fold_uint_max=4294967295
fold_uint_complement=4294967295
fold_uint_underflow=4294967295
fold_uint_overflow=0
fold_uint_mul_wrap=0
fold_uint_mul_max=4294967293
fold_uchar_wrap=0
fold_ushort_wrap=0
fold_ullong_underflow=18446744073709551615
fold_ullong_overflow=0
fold_modular_identity=1
run_uint_max=4294967295
run_uint_complement=4294967295
run_uint_underflow=4294967295
run_uint_overflow=0
run_uint_mul_wrap=0
run_uint_mul_max=4294967293
run_uchar_wrap=0
run_ushort_wrap=0
run_ullong_underflow=18446744073709551615
run_ullong_overflow=0
run_modular_identity=1
END
